When a writing suggestion shows, Ladybird splits the box of the caret’s
text node at the caret, around an anonymous inline box that holds the
suggestion, drawn in the text color at half alpha. WebKit does the same:
RenderTreeUpdater::GeneratedContent::updateWritingSuggestionsRenderer()
splits the caret’s text renderer into a prefix, a suffix, and between
them an ::-internal-writing-suggestions inline box, whose UA style is
color-mix(currentColor 50%, transparent).

In WebKit, WebViewImpl::requestCandidatesForSelectionIfNeeded() asks
NSSpellChecker for candidates, and showInlinePredictionsForCandidates()
picks one. WebViewImpl::setMarkedText() then hands completion-tagged
text to WebPageProxy::setWritingSuggestion(), and WebCore’s Editor::
setWritingSuggestion() shows it without touching the DOM. Accepting it
is AppKit inserting the text, so the page sees an insertText input
event, just as it does in Ladybird.

WebKit offers a suggestion only where both VisibleSelection::
canEnableWritingSuggestions() and Element::isWritingSuggestionsEnabled()
allow one. Ladybird’s WritingSuggestionController::is_eligible() follows
the latter, including its autocomplete=off check. But it also allows
the Telephone input type, which the spec now lists and WebKit’s
HTMLInputElement::supportsWritingSuggestions() doesn’t.

WebKit keeps the suggestion out of its accessibility text runs, in
AccessibilityRenderObject’s shouldExcludeTextRunsForPseudoElement().
Ladybird’s accessibility tree is built from the DOM, so the suggestion
never reaches that tree at all.

Neither Blink nor Gecko shows macOS inline predictions in web content.
The names NSTextCompletionAttributeName, showCompletionForCandidate, and
isAutomaticInlineCompletionEnabled appear nowhere in their sources.
